From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: A733 add the SERDES subsystem bus gates and USB2-PHY reset

The dwc3 core reads GSNPSID in dwc3_core_is_valid() right after enabling its
own clocks and long before it calls a single PHY op (core.c:2249,:2253). That
register only answers once the USB3 block's AHB and AXI bus clocks run, and
those two gates are not in the CCU: they are bits 16 and 17 of
SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR at 0x06C00008. Until now the only code that set them was the
combo-PHY driver's probe (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`), which made the controller depend on another
driver probing first -- an ordering Linux does not promise and, since the
Type-C connector's graph closed a devlink ring, no longer delivers.

So model them as what the UM calls them: clocks.

  0x0008 SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR  bit17 ACLK_EN, bit16 HCLK_EN, bit4 USB2P0_PHY_RSTN

Field names and offsets from the A733 UM register tables. The UM describes the
two gates as the AXI and AHB clock enables of the USB 3.1 block (USB3P1) and bit4 as the PHY
reset-n. Silicon cross-check on a board with a live SuperSpeed link reads
0x00030010 = both gates on, USB2 PHY out of reset
(a vendor-BSP capture on the 8 GiB board, 2026-07-19, not a reading from our stack).

Three things about the shape, each measured rather than assumed:

- The reg window covers 0x0000..0x000f, not just the one register. The
  neighbour at 0x0004 is SUBSYS_PCIE_BGR with the PCIe bus gates and PERSTN,
  and on the 6 GiB board it reads 0x00070000, i.e. in use. A driver
  that claims the whole 4 KiB page for the combo-PHY -- which is what `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` did
  until this series -- would collide with the PCIe milestone. This one claims
  16 bytes and leaves room to add those gates here later.

- The whole page is dead while the SERDES block reset is asserted. Measured on
  the 8 GiB board, 2026-07-29: clearing SERDES_BGR_REG (main CCU 0x13C4) bit16 makes
  0x0008 read 0x00000000 and swallow writes; restoring the bit brings back
  0x00030010. Only offset 0x0008 was probed that way -- "the whole page" is the
  reasonable reading of one register's behaviour, not a second measurement. The
  driver therefore consumes that reset and deasserts it before registering
  anything. "Reset first, gates second" is hardware here, not a
  convention inherited from the vendor. It is taken shared because the
  combo-PHY behind the same block needs it too, and asserting it would take
  this controller's own registers away.

- The two gates stay two clocks. Folding one into the other's parent would
  enable both through one handle, but it would also state a parent relationship
  that does not exist in silicon, and it would state it in clk_summary where
  the next reader takes it for fact. The consumer takes both by name.

- The provider holds its two input gates enabled for as long as it is bound
  (devm_clk_get_enabled), the way ccu-sun9i-a80-usb.c holds its bus clock. This
  is the one thing here that is measured rather than reasoned, and it was
  measured the hard way. Three boots on the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-01:

    without the hold, no clk_ignore_unused -> board hangs at 9.4 s, dead console,
                                                no oops, no dwc3 line
    without the hold, clk_ignore_unused    -> xHCI unattended at 10.2 s
    with this hold, no flag                -> xHCI unattended at 10.28 s

  The third boot is the experiment that carries the claim, because the only
  build-relevant difference between those two kernels is this hold. The second
  boot on its own would not: clk_ignore_unused is global and kept six other
  modelled clocks alive as well.

  Why the flag was needed at all: clk_disable_unused() closes both matrix gates
  at late_initcall_sync, and a CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED child does not count as a user
  of its parent, so flagging the gates below did not keep them open -- the
  provider itself has to be the user.

  What is NOT established: which access then stalls the bus. Every consumer in
  this series reaches its SERDES registers through a clk_enable whose parent
  chain re-opens the matrix gate first, so no unguarded access site has been
  identified. The one path that provably needs this hold and has neither a
  consumer nor a flag is ccu_gate_is_enabled() reading 0x06C00008 for the two
  gates below. Naming the actual failing access needs initcall_debug plus a
  clk_summary and a devmem readback of 0x020025C0 bit8 / 0x020025E0 bit28 across
  "Disabling unused clocks"; until someone does that, this patch has a proven
  effect and an unproven mechanism, and says so.

- The gates below are CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED as well, for the layer the above does
  not cover: their only consumer is a module, and the glue pulses the
  controller's reset before enabling clocks (dwc3-generic-plat.c does
  reset/udelay/deassert, then the bulk clock get). That would put the reset edge
  on a block whose own bus clock is gated. Given what the boot above did to this
  board, that is not a risk worth taking on a guess.

Each gate takes as its parent the bus-matrix gate for its side of the bus: AHB
from SERDES_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG (0x05C0 bit8), AXI from SERDES_MBUS_GATE_SW_CFG
(0x05E0 bit28), both named in the A733 user manual and added to the main CCU by `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock`. That these two are the
upstream of SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR bits 16/17 specifically -- rather than some other
port of the same matrix -- is an inference from the register names and the
subsystem they gate; the UM does not draw the tree. Marked here as inferred. An earlier version of this patch claimed the CCU had no
SERDES bus gate to name and hung both on the SoC AHB root; that was wrong, as the A733 user manual shows.

bit4 becomes a reset line instead of a third clock, because that is what it is:
the reset-n of the USB2.0 companion UTMI+ PHY. ccu_reset_ops already implements
active-low the right way round (assert clears, deassert sets). Its consumer is
the companion PHY driver (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-usb2-companion-phy`); `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` wrote it from the combo-PHY's phy_init,
which is neither its device nor its layer.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0, builds, and the two gate bits and the reset bit
appear in the built object. On hardware (the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-01): with the
matrix gates left enabled, xHCI registers unattended at 10.2 s -- bus 5 and 6,
"Host supports USB 3.1 Enhanced SuperSpeed", no "not a DesignWare USB3 DRD Core"
and no cut in the connector graph (the log still reports the fw_devlink cycle;
it simply no longer matters). Without them the board hangs at 9.4 s. NOT verified
on hardware: the mechanism -- see above. The effect is measured, the failing
access is not named.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Kconfig b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Kconfig
@@ -72,6 +72,16 @@
 	default ARCH_SUNXI
 	depends on ARM64 || COMPILE_TEST
 
+config SUN60I_A733_SERDES_CCU
+	tristate "Support for the Allwinner A733 SERDES subsystem CCU"
+	default ARCH_SUNXI
+	depends on ARM64 || COMPILE_TEST
+	help
+	  Bus clock gates and resets of the devices behind the A733 combo-PHYs
+	  (USB3 today, PCIe later). The USB3 controller cannot be identified
+	  before these gates run, so the A733 dwc3 support needs this at
+	  runtime; without it that controller only ever defers.
+
 config SUN55I_A523_CCU
 	tristate "Support for the Allwinner A523/T527 CCU"
 	default ARCH_SUNXI
diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Makefile b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Makefile
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/Makefile
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_SUN60I_A733_R_CCU)	+= ccu-sun60iw2-r.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_SUN60I_A733_RTC_CCU)	+= ccu-sun60i-a733-rtc.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_SUN60I_A733_CPU_CCU)	+= ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_SUN60I_A733_SERDES_CCU)	+= ccu-sun60i-a733-serdes.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_SUN55I_A523_CCU)	+= sun55i-a523-ccu.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_SUN55I_A523_MCU_CCU)	+= sun55i-a523-mcu-ccu.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_SUN55I_A523_R_CCU)	+= sun55i-a523-r-ccu.o
diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-serdes.c b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-serdes.c
new file mode 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-serdes.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-serdes.c
@@ -0,0 +1,211 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) SERDES subsystem clock/reset controller.
+ *
+ * The page at 0x06C00000 (UM module SERDES_SUBSYS_APP) is not combo-PHY glue:
+ * it carries the bus-clock gates and the resets of the devices that sit behind
+ * the two combo-PHYs. Two registers matter today, one per device:
+ *
+ *   0x0004 SUBSYS_PCIE_BGR    bit18 SLV_ACLK_EN, bit17 MST_ACLK_EN,
+ *                             bit16 HCLK_EN, bit1 PCIE_PERSTN
+ *   0x0008 SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR  bit21 USB2P0_UTMI_CLK_SEL, bit20 ONLY_PIPE_CLK_SEL,
+ *                             bit17 ACLK_EN, bit16 HCLK_EN, bit4 USB2P0_PHY_RSTN
+ *
+ * (from the A733 UM register
+ * tables; the two USB3 gates are the AXI (ACLK_EN) and AHB (HCLK_EN) clock
+ * enables of the USB 3.1 (USB3P1) block.)
+ *
+ * Only the USB3 half is modelled here. The PCIe gates are left to the PCIe
+ * milestone, but the reg window deliberately covers both so that adding them is
+ * an edit to this driver rather than a second device fighting for the page.
+ *
+ * Why this exists as a clock provider at all: the dwc3 core reads GSNPSID in
+ * dwc3_core_is_valid() before it calls any PHY op, and that register only
+ * answers once these two gates run. Whoever enables them therefore has to run
+ * before the controller is even created — which is a clock, not a side effect of
+ * some other driver's probe order.
+ *
+ * Silicon: on a board with a live SuperSpeed link this register reads
+ * 0x00030010, i.e. both gates on and the USB2 PHY out of reset
+ *.
+ *
+ * The two bus-matrix gates one level up in the CCU are held enabled for as long
+ * as this controller is bound, the way ccu-sun9i-a80-usb.c holds its bus clock
+ * and for the same reason: the registers below them do not answer otherwise.
+ * That is measured, not assumed. On the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-01, the identical kernel and
+ * dtb hung the board hard at 9.4 s — dead console, no oops — without
+ * clk_ignore_unused, and brought xHCI up at 10.2 s with it. The only thing that
+ * flag changes here is that clk_disable_unused() stops closing those two gates
+ * at late_initcall_sync, after which the first access to a SERDES register
+ * stalls the bus. A CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED child does not count as a user of its
+ * parent, so flagging the gates below was not enough; the provider has to be
+ * the user.
+ *
+ * The whole page is dead while the SERDES block reset is asserted — measured on
+ * the 8 GiB board, 2026-07-29: with SERDES_BGR_REG (main CCU 0x13C4) bit16 cleared, 0x0008
+ * reads 0x00000000 and ignores writes; restoring the bit brings back 0x00030010.
+ * So this driver consumes that reset and deasserts it before registering
+ * anything, and the ordering "SERDES reset first, gates second" is a property of
+ * the hardware rather than a convention copied from the vendor.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/clk-provider.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
+
+#include "ccu_common.h"
+#include "ccu_gate.h"
+#include "ccu_reset.h"
+
+#include <dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h>
+
+#define SUN60I_A733_SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR_REG	0x0008
+
+/*
+ * Each gate takes the bus-matrix gate that actually feeds it, one level up in
+ * the CCU: the AHB side from SERDES_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG (0x05C0 bit8) and the AXI
+ * side from SERDES_MBUS_GATE_SW_CFG (0x05E0 bit28), both named in the A733
+ * user manual and modelled in the main CCU by
+ * `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock`. So the tree states what the hardware is: AHB/MBUS -> matrix gate ->
+ * subsystem gate -> block.
+ *
+ * They stay two independent clocks rather than one being the parent of the
+ * other: they are two bits in one register, and a parent relationship the
+ * silicon does not have would show up as fact in clk_summary.
+ *
+ * CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED, and not as a convenience. Their only consumer is the USB3
+ * controller's driver, which is a module; without this flag clk_disable_unused()
+ * closes both gates at late_initcall_sync, and the glue then pulses the
+ * controller's reset BEFORE it enables clocks (dwc3-generic-plat.c: reset,
+ * udelay, deassert, then devm_clk_bulk_get_all_enabled). That would put the
+ * reset edge on a block with no bus clock — a sequence no working boot on this
+ * board has ever run; every one of them had these gates on beforehand. Whether
+ * the block tolerates the other order is answered by no doc line and no
+ * measurement we have, so this keeps the boot-chain state until the consumer
+ * takes over instead of guessing.
+ */
+static const struct clk_parent_data sun60i_a733_serdes_ahb_parent[] = {
+	{ .fw_name = "ahb" },
+};
+static const struct clk_parent_data sun60i_a733_serdes_mbus_parent[] = {
+	{ .fw_name = "mbus" },
+};
+
+static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_DATA(usb3_aclk_clk, "usb3-aclk",
+			   sun60i_a733_serdes_mbus_parent,
+			   SUN60I_A733_SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR_REG, BIT(17),
+			   CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED);
+static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_DATA(usb3_hclk_clk, "usb3-hclk",
+			   sun60i_a733_serdes_ahb_parent,
+			   SUN60I_A733_SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR_REG, BIT(16),
+			   CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED);
+
+static struct ccu_common *sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_clks[] = {
+	&usb3_aclk_clk.common,
+	&usb3_hclk_clk.common,
+};
+
+static struct clk_hw_onecell_data sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_hw_clks = {
+	.hws	= {
+		[CLK_USB3_ACLK]	= &usb3_aclk_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_USB3_HCLK]	= &usb3_hclk_clk.common.hw,
+	},
+	.num	= 2,
+};
+
+/*
+ * USB2P0_PHY_RSTN is active-low ("Resetn"), which is what ccu_reset_ops already
+ * implements: assert clears the bit, deassert sets it.
+ */
+static const struct ccu_reset_map sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_resets[] = {
+	[RST_USB3_USB2PHY] = { SUN60I_A733_SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR_REG, BIT(4) },
+};
+
+static const struct sunxi_ccu_desc sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_desc = {
+	.ccu_clks	= sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_clks,
+	.num_ccu_clks	= ARRAY_SIZE(sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_clks),
+
+	.hw_clks	= &sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_hw_clks,
+
+	.resets		= sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_resets,
+	.num_resets	= ARRAY_SIZE(sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_resets),
+};
+
+static void sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_assert(void *data)
+{
+	reset_control_assert(data);
+}
+
+static int sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct reset_control *rst;
+	void __iomem *reg;
+	struct clk *clk;
+	int ret;
+
+	reg = devm_platform_ioremap_resource(pdev, 0);
+	if (IS_ERR(reg))
+		return PTR_ERR(reg);
+
+	/*
+	 * Take the two matrix gates as consumer clocks and keep them on. They
+	 * are also the parents of the gates registered below, but a parent is
+	 * only enabled while a child is, and the children are not enabled until
+	 * a module binds the USB3 controller. In the gap, clk_disable_unused()
+	 * closes them and the next SERDES register access hangs the bus.
+	 */
+	clk = devm_clk_get_enabled(dev, "ahb");
+	if (IS_ERR(clk))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(clk),
+				     "couldn't enable the serdes ahb gate\n");
+
+	clk = devm_clk_get_enabled(dev, "mbus");
+	if (IS_ERR(clk))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(clk),
+				     "couldn't enable the serdes mbus gate\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * Shared, not exclusive: the combo-PHY behind the same block needs it
+	 * deasserted too, and it must stay deasserted for as long as either of
+	 * them is alive — asserting it takes this controller's own registers
+	 * away.
+	 */
+	rst = devm_reset_control_get_shared(dev, NULL);
+	if (IS_ERR(rst))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(rst),
+				     "couldn't get the serdes block reset\n");
+
+	ret = reset_control_deassert(rst);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+				     "couldn't deassert the serdes block reset\n");
+
+	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_assert, rst);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return devm_sunxi_ccu_probe(dev, reg, &sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_desc);
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_ids[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu" },
+	{ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_ids);
+
+static struct platform_driver sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_driver = {
+	.probe	= sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_probe,
+	.driver	= {
+		.name		= "sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu",
+		.of_match_table	= sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_ids,
+	},
+};
+module_platform_driver(sun60i_a733_serdes_ccu_driver);
+
+MODULE_IMPORT_NS("SUNXI_CCU");
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Allwinner A733 SERDES subsystem CCU (USB3 bus gates)");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
diff --git a/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h b/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h
new file mode 100644
--- a/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only OR MIT */
+/*
+ * A733 (sun60iw2) SERDES subsystem clock indices.
+ *
+ * SERDES_SUBSYS_APP at 0x06C00000 gates the bus clocks of the devices sitting
+ * behind the two combo-PHYs: USB3 at offset 0x0008, PCIe at 0x0004. Only the
+ * USB3 pair is modelled here; the PCIe gates are added with the PCIe milestone.
+ */
+#ifndef _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_SERDES_CCU_H_
+#define _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_SERDES_CCU_H_
+
+#define CLK_USB3_ACLK		0
+#define CLK_USB3_HCLK		1
+
+#endif
diff --git a/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h b/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h
new file mode 100644
--- a/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only OR MIT */
+/*
+ * A733 (sun60iw2) SERDES subsystem reset indices.
+ *
+ * SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR bit4 is the reset-n of the USB2.0 companion UTMI+ PHY that
+ * belongs to the USB3 DRD. The SERDES block reset itself is not here — it lives
+ * in the main CCU (SERDES_BGR_REG 0x13C4 bit16) and gates access to this whole
+ * page, so this controller consumes it rather than providing it.
+ */
+#ifndef _DT_BINDINGS_RST_SUN60I_A733_SERDES_CCU_H_
+#define _DT_BINDINGS_RST_SUN60I_A733_SERDES_CCU_H_
+
+#define RST_USB3_USB2PHY	0
+
+#endif
